## Q7 — Commutative Matrices (4 marks)
**Spec ref**: C1
### Question
The matrices A and B are defined as:
$$A = \begin{pmatrix} p-2 & p-1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}, \quad B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2p-1 \\ 0 & 4-p \end{pmatrix}$$
Find the values of p such that A and B are commutative under matrix multiplication.
Fully justify your answer.
### Solution
For commutativity, AB = BA.
**AB:**
$$AB = \begin{pmatrix} p-2 & p-1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2p-1 \\ 0 & 4-p \end{pmatrix}$$
$$= \begin{pmatrix} (p-2)(1) + (p-1)(0) & (p-2)(2p-1) + (p-1)(4-p) \\ 0 & 0(2p-1) + 1(4-p) \end{pmatrix}$$
$$= \begin{pmatrix} p-2 & (p-2)(2p-1) + (p-1)(4-p) \\ 0 & 4-p \end{pmatrix}$$
Top-right entry of AB:
$(p-2)(2p-1) + (p-1)(4-p)$
$= 2p^2 - p - 4p + 2 + 4p - p^2 - 4 + p$
$= p^2 + 0p - 2$
$= p^2 - 2$
**BA:**
$$BA = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2p-1 \\ 0 & 4-p \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} p-2 & p-1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$$
$$= \begin{pmatrix} p-2 & (p-1) + (2p-1) \\ 0 & 4-p \end{pmatrix}$$
$$= \begin{pmatrix} p-2 & 3p-2 \\ 0 & 4-p \end{pmatrix}$$
For AB = BA, we need the top-right entries to be equal (other entries already match):
$$p^2 - 2 = 3p - 2$$
$$p^2 - 3p = 0$$
$$p(p - 3) = 0$$
$$p = 0 \text{ or } p = 3$$
### Answer
**p = 0 or p = 3**
