## Q11 — Polynomial Roots Reasoning (3 marks)
**Spec ref**: B3
### Question
Latifa and Sam are studying polynomial equations of degree greater than 2, with real coefficients and no repeated roots.
Latifa says that if such an equation has exactly one real root, it must be of degree 3.
Sam says that this is not correct.
State, giving reasons, whether Latifa or Sam is right.
### Solution
**Sam is right.**
Complex roots of polynomials with real coefficients occur in conjugate pairs. Therefore, the number of non-real roots is always even.
A polynomial of degree n has exactly n roots (counting multiplicity). If it has exactly 1 real root, then it has (n − 1) non-real roots. Since non-real roots come in pairs, (n − 1) must be even, so n must be **odd**.
Latifa claims n must be 3, but any odd degree greater than 2 works. For example, a polynomial of degree **5** could have exactly 1 real root and 4 complex roots (2 conjugate pairs).
Example: (x − 1)(x² + 1)(x² + 4) = 0 is degree 5 with exactly one real root (x = 1).
