## Q12 — Composite Transformations (5 marks)
**Spec ref**: C3
### Question
The transformation S is represented by the matrix:
$$M = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & -6 \\ 2 & 7 \end{pmatrix}$$
The transformation T is a reflection in the line y = x√3 and is represented by the matrix N.
The point P(x, y) is transformed first by S, then by T.
The result of these transformations is the point Q(3, 8).
Find the coordinates of P. Give answers to 3 d.p.
### Solution
The composite transformation is T(S(P)) = Q, so NMP = Q, which means P = M⁻¹N⁻¹Q.
**Step 1: Find N.**
The line y = x√3 has gradient √3, so makes angle 60° with the positive x-axis (tan⁻¹(√3) = 60°).
The reflection matrix for a line through the origin at angle θ is:
$$N = \begin{pmatrix} \cos 2\theta & \sin 2\theta \\ \sin 2\theta & -\cos 2\theta \end{pmatrix}$$
With θ = 60°:
$$N = \begin{pmatrix} \cos 120° & \sin 120° \\ \sin 120° & -\cos 120° \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1/2 & \sqrt{3}/2 \\ \sqrt{3}/2 & 1/2 \end{pmatrix}$$
Since N is a reflection, N⁻¹ = N (reflections are self-inverse).
**Step 2: Find N⁻¹Q = NQ.**
$$NQ = \begin{pmatrix} -1/2 & \sqrt{3}/2 \\ \sqrt{3}/2 & 1/2 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 8 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -3/2 + 4\sqrt{3} \\ 3\sqrt{3}/2 + 4 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 5.42820... \\ 6.59808... \end{pmatrix}$$
**Step 3: Find M⁻¹.**
det(M) = (1)(7) − (−6)(2) = 7 + 12 = 19
$$M^{-1} = \frac{1}{19}\begin{pmatrix} 7 & 6 \\ -2 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$$
**Step 4: Find P = M⁻¹(NQ).**
$$P = \frac{1}{19}\begin{pmatrix} 7 & 6 \\ -2 & 1 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 5.42820... \\ 6.59808... \end{pmatrix}$$
$$= \frac{1}{19}\begin{pmatrix} 37.9974... + 39.5885... \\ -10.8564... + 6.59808... \end{pmatrix}$$
$$= \frac{1}{19}\begin{pmatrix} 77.5859... \\ -4.25833... \end{pmatrix}$$
$$= \begin{pmatrix} 4.08347... \\ -0.22412... \end{pmatrix}$$
### Answer
**P = (4.083, −0.224)** (to 3 d.p.)
