## Q14 — Inverse Matrix and Simultaneous Equations (10 marks)
**Spec ref**: C6, C7, C8
### Question
The matrix M is defined as:
$$M = \begin{pmatrix} 5 & 2 & 1 \\ 6 & 3 & 2k+3 \\ 2 & 1 & 5 \end{pmatrix}$$
where k is a constant.
**(a)** Given that M is non-singular, find M⁻¹ in terms of k. [5 marks]
**(b)** State any restrictions on the value of k. [1 mark]
**(c)** Using your answer to part (a), show that the solution to the set of simultaneous equations below is independent of the value of k:
5x + 2y + z = 1  
6x + 3y + (2k + 3)z = 4k + 3  
2x + y + 5z = 9
[4 marks]
### Solution
**(a)**
det(M) = 5(15 − (2k+3)) − 2(30 − 2(2k+3)) + 1(6 − 6)
= 5(15 − 2k − 3) − 2(30 − 4k − 6) + 0
= 5(12 − 2k) − 2(24 − 4k)
= 60 − 10k − 48 + 8k
= 12 − 2k
= **2(6 − k)**
The matrix of cofactors:
C₁₁ = +(3·5 − (2k+3)·1) = 15 − 2k − 3 = 12 − 2k  
C₁₂ = −(6·5 − (2k+3)·2) = −(30 − 4k − 6) = −(24 − 4k) = 4k − 24  
C₁₃ = +(6·1 − 3·2) = 6 − 6 = 0
C₂₁ = −(2·5 − 1·1) = −(10 − 1) = −9  
C₂₂ = +(5·5 − 1·2) = 25 − 2 = 23  
C₂₃ = −(5·1 − 2·2) = −(5 − 4) = −1
C₃₁ = +(2(2k+3) − 3·1) = 4k + 6 − 3 = 4k + 3  
C₃₂ = −(5(2k+3) − 6·1) = −(10k + 15 − 6) = −(10k + 9)  
C₃₃ = +(5·3 − 2·6) = 15 − 12 = 3
Adjugate (transpose of cofactor matrix):
$$\text{adj}(M) = \begin{pmatrix} 12-2k & -9 & 4k+3 \\ 4k-24 & 23 & -(10k+9) \\ 0 & -1 & 3 \end{pmatrix}$$
$$M^{-1} = \frac{1}{2(6-k)}\begin{pmatrix} 12-2k & -9 & 4k+3 \\ 4k-24 & 23 & -(10k+9) \\ 0 & -1 & 3 \end{pmatrix}$$
**(b)**
For M to be non-singular: det(M) ≠ 0, so 2(6 − k) ≠ 0.
**k ≠ 6**
**(c)**
The system Mx = b where b = (1, 4k+3, 9)ᵀ.
x = M⁻¹b:
$$\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = \frac{1}{2(6-k)}\begin{pmatrix} 12-2k & -9 & 4k+3 \\ 4k-24 & 23 & -(10k+9) \\ 0 & -1 & 3 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4k+3 \\ 9 \end{pmatrix}$$
**Top row (for x):**
(12−2k)(1) + (−9)(4k+3) + (4k+3)(9)
= 12 − 2k − 36k − 27 + 36k + 27
= 12 − 2k
= 2(6 − k)
So x = 2(6−k) / (2(6−k)) = **1**
**Middle row (for y):**
(4k−24)(1) + (23)(4k+3) + (−(10k+9))(9)
= 4k − 24 + 92k + 69 − 90k − 81
= (4k + 92k − 90k) + (−24 + 69 − 81)
= 6k − 36
= 6(k − 6) = −6(6 − k)
So y = −6(6−k) / (2(6−k)) = **−3**
**Bottom row (for z):**
(0)(1) + (−1)(4k+3) + (3)(9)
= 0 − 4k − 3 + 27
= 24 − 4k
= 4(6 − k)
So z = 4(6−k) / (2(6−k)) = **2**
Since x = 1, y = −3, z = 2 for all valid k, the solution is **independent of k**. □
