## Q15 — Modulus Inequalities and Graphs (7 marks)
**Spec ref**: D10, D11
### Question
**(a)** On the diagram showing y = 5 − x, sketch the graph of y = |x² − 4x|, including all parts where it intersects y = 5 − x. [3 marks]
**(b)** Find the solution of the inequality |x² − 4x| > 5 − x. Give your answer in exact form. [4 marks]
### Solution
**(a)** Sketch description:
y = x² − 4x = x(x − 4), which is a parabola with roots at x = 0 and x = 4, vertex at (2, −4).
y = |x² − 4x|: the portion below the x-axis (0 < x < 4) is reflected above. The graph:
- For x ≤ 0: y = x² − 4x (original, already ≥ 0)
- For 0 ≤ x ≤ 4: y = −(x² − 4x) = −x² + 4x (reflected, with maximum at (2, 4))
- For x ≥ 4: y = x² − 4x (original, already ≥ 0)
**(b)**
**Case 1**: x² − 4x > 5 − x (where x² − 4x ≥ 0, i.e., x ≤ 0 or x ≥ 4)
x² − 4x > 5 − x  
x² − 3x − 5 > 0  
x = (3 ± √29)/2
So x < (3 − √29)/2 or x > (3 + √29)/2
Combined with x ≤ 0 or x ≥ 4:
- x < (3 − √29)/2 (since (3 − √29)/2 ≈ −1.19, this is within x ≤ 0) ✓
- x > (3 + √29)/2 (since (3 + √29)/2 ≈ 4.19, this is within x ≥ 4) ✓
**Case 2**: −(x² − 4x) > 5 − x (where x² − 4x < 0, i.e., 0 < x < 4)
−x² + 4x > 5 − x  
−x² + 5x − 5 > 0  
x² − 5x + 5 < 0  
x = (5 ± √5)/2
So (5 − √5)/2 < x < (5 + √5)/2
Combined with 0 < x < 4:
- (5 − √5)/2 ≈ 1.38 and (5 + √5)/2 ≈ 3.62
- Both within (0, 4), so: (5 − √5)/2 < x < (5 + √5)/2 ✓
### Answer
$$x < \frac{3 - \sqrt{29}}{2} \quad \text{or} \quad \frac{5 - \sqrt{5}}{2} < x < \frac{5 + \sqrt{5}}{2} \quad \text{or} \quad x > \frac{3 + \sqrt{29}}{2}$$
