## Q16 — Rational Functions and Volumes of Revolution (9 marks)
**Spec ref**: D12, E2
### Question
The function f is defined by:
$$f(x) = \frac{ax + 5}{x + b}$$
The graph of y = f(x) has asymptotes x = −2 and y = 3.
**(a)** Write down the value of a and the value of b. [2 marks]
**(b)(i)** The shaded region R is enclosed by y = f(x), the x-axis and the y-axis. R is rotated 360° about the x-axis. Find the volume. Give to 3 s.f. [3 marks]
**(b)(ii)** R is rotated 360° about the y-axis. Find the volume. Give to 3 s.f. [4 marks]
### Solution
**(a)**
Vertical asymptote at x = −2 ⟹ x + b = 0 when x = −2, so **b = 2**.
Horizontal asymptote: as x → ∞, f(x) → a. Since y = 3 is the asymptote, **a = 3**.
So f(x) = (3x + 5)/(x + 2).
**(b)(i)** Volume about x-axis:
The x-intercept: 3x + 5 = 0 → x = −5/3.
The region R is between x = −5/3 and x = 0 (where the curve is between the x-axis and itself), bounded by y-axis.
Wait — actually, the region R is enclosed by y = f(x), the x-axis and the y-axis. Let me find where the curve crosses the axes.
At x = 0: y = 5/2 = 2.5  
At y = 0: 3x + 5 = 0 → x = −5/3
So R is bounded by: curve from x = −5/3 to x = 0, x-axis, and y-axis.
$$V = \pi \int_{-5/3}^{0} \left(\frac{3x+5}{x+2}\right)^2 dx$$
Write (3x + 5)/(x + 2) = 3 − 1/(x + 2):
$$\left(3 - \frac{1}{x+2}\right)^2 = 9 - \frac{6}{x+2} + \frac{1}{(x+2)^2}$$
$$V = \pi \int_{-5/3}^{0} \left(9 - \frac{6}{x+2} + \frac{1}{(x+2)^2}\right) dx$$
$$= \pi \left[9x - 6\ln|x+2| - \frac{1}{x+2}\right]_{-5/3}^{0}$$
At x = 0: 0 − 6 ln 2 − 1/2 = −6 ln 2 − 0.5
At x = −5/3: 9(−5/3) − 6 ln(1/3) − 1/(1/3) = −15 − 6 ln(1/3) − 3 = −18 + 6 ln 3
$$V = \pi[(-6\ln 2 - 0.5) - (-18 + 6\ln 3)]$$
$$= \pi[17.5 - 6\ln 2 - 6\ln 3]$$
$$= \pi[17.5 - 6\ln 6]$$
$$= \pi(17.5 - 10.7506...)$$
$$= \pi(6.7494...)$$
$$= 21.2028...$$
### Answer (b)(i)
**V ≈ 21.2 cubic units** (to 3 s.f.)
**(b)(ii)** Volume about y-axis:
Using the shell method or rearranging. Since rotating about y-axis:
From f(x) = (3x + 5)/(x + 2), rearrange for x in terms of y:
y(x + 2) = 3x + 5  
xy + 2y = 3x + 5  
x(y − 3) = 5 − 2y  
x = (5 − 2y)/(y − 3)
At x = −5/3: y = 0  
At x = 0: y = 5/2
$$V = \pi \int_{0}^{5/2} x^2 \, dy = \pi \int_{0}^{5/2} \left(\frac{5 - 2y}{y - 3}\right)^2 dy$$
Write (5 − 2y)/(y − 3) = −2 − 1/(y − 3):
$$= \pi \int_{0}^{5/2} \left(4 + \frac{4}{y-3} + \frac{1}{(y-3)^2}\right) dy$$
$$= \pi \left[4y + 4\ln|y-3| - \frac{1}{y-3}\right]_{0}^{5/2}$$
At y = 5/2: 10 + 4 ln(1/2) − 1/(−1/2) = 10 − 4 ln 2 + 2 = 12 − 4 ln 2
At y = 0: 0 + 4 ln 3 − 1/(−3) = 4 ln 3 + 1/3
$$V = \pi[(12 - 4\ln 2) - (4\ln 3 + 1/3)]$$
$$= \pi[35/3 - 4\ln 6]$$
$$= \pi[11.6667 - 7.1671]$$
$$= \pi(4.4996...)$$
$$= 14.137...$$
### Answer (b)(ii)
**V ≈ 14.1 cubic units** (to 3 s.f.)
